                <pb facs="0361" n="354" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg354"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9241: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-07T22:00:00+08:00">August 7, 1937—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 7—12:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>388. 1. Following statement in English was released for the press by the Foreign
                    Office at 9 o’clock this evening.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“Interviewed by newspapermen today, a spokesman of the Foreign Office stated
                        that he had no definite information whether Mr. Shigeru Kawagoe was coming
                        immediately to Nanking from Shanghai, although when the Japanese Ambassador
                        left for Tientsin he told Chinese officers of his intention to return
                        southward after about a month’s absence.</p>
                    <p>Questioned concerning the Government’s attitude in the event of Mr. Kawagoe
                        desiring to open negotiations with Nanking, the spokesman said that such
                        negotiations might be entered into at any time so long as diplomatic
                        relations continued to exist between China and Japan.</p>
                    <p>He emphasized however that Sino-Japanese relations had reached such a
                        critical stage that the issue of peace or war would have to be determined at
                        this very next moment.</p>
                    <p>‘It is still not too late to avert a disastrous conflict by a strong
                        determination and the greatest efforts on the part of Japan toward that
                        end,’ the spokesman declared.</p>
                    <p>‘Failing such efforts’ he added, ‘it would be difficult to discover any ray
                        of hope for peace’”.</p>
                </quote>
                <p>2. Sent to the Department, Peiping, Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
